Z||000000||Coop88||05-28-2001||06:27 PM||||I was checking out my e-brake and noticed that one caliper has a lot more play than the other. I took the springs off and disconnected the cables. I can push the left rear e-brake lever(on the caliper) foreward about a half inch with my thumb before it starts to squeeze the rotor. On the right side, I can push the lever almost all the way foreward. I'm thinking this is the reason my brake won't hold-only one caliper squeezing the rotor? I've tried pumping the brakes and turning the levers with a wrench, but it didn't make a difference. Are the brakes supposed to be this way, or do I need to tighten the right caliper? How would I do this? Both calipers and cables were replaced last March.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||05-28-2001||08:47 PM||||The lever should ratchet the piston up snugly. When the pads were replaced did the pins on the pad line up with the slots in the piston properly?||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||05-28-2001||08:48 PM|||| They should automatically adjust to compensate for brake pad wear.  Mine do!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Coop88||05-28-2001||10:05 PM||||I unbolted the right rear caliper for a better look. When I push the e-brake lever foreward, the piston doesn't move until it gets to the end. The piston moves less than 1/16th of an inch. On the left side, the piston starts to move after the lever has been pushed 1/2". How do I fix the right side caliper? Do I just unbolt the brake lever and turn the piston out a little?||||reg||10
